{"input_text": "Question: I have a sequence $a_{n} = \binom{2n}{n}$ and I need to check whether this sequence converges to a limit without finding the limit itself. Now I tried to calculate $a_{n+1}$ but it doesn't get me anywhere. I think I can show somehow that $a_{n}$ is always increasing and that it has no upper bound, but I'm not sure if that's the right way 

Answer: For each $n$-set $A\subset[2n]$ we can form two different $(n+1)$-sets $$A':=A\cup\{2n+1\},\ A'':=A\cup\{2n+2\}\quad\subset [2(n+1)]\ .$$This implies ${2(n+1)\choose n+1}\geq 2{2n\choose n}$, hence $a_n\to\infty$ $(n\to\infty)$. 
