Answer: As is the case here the transformation $z = g(x) = x^2$ is one-to-one over the support and has the inverse transformation 
 $$x = g^{-1}(z) = h(z) = \sqrt{z}$$ 
 and then as already has been said in the other answers the pdf of $Z$ is given by 
 $$f_Z(z) = f_X(x)\left|\frac{dx}{dz}\right| = f_X[h(z)]\left|\frac{dh(z)}{dz}\right| = f_X[\sqrt{z}]\left|\frac{d\sqrt{z}}{dz}\right| \:\:\:\:\:(1)$$ 
 In the general case, i.e. when you are not so lucky that it is one-to-one over the support it gets a little bit more complicated. 
 Denoting the real roots of $z = g(x)$ by $x_k$ then  
 $$f_Z(z) = \sum_k \frac{f_X(x_k)}{|g'(x_k)|} \:\:\:\:\: (2)$$ 
 In this case with $z = g(x) = x^2:$ 
 If $z<0$, then the equation $z = x^2$ has no real solutions; hence, $f_Z(z)=0$.  
 If $z>0$, then $z=x^2$ has two solutions, $x_1 = \sqrt{z}$ and $x_2 = -\sqrt{z}$.  
 Further we have $g'(x)=2x$. Hence by the transformation of variables formula $(2)$:
$$
f_Z(z) =
\begin{cases}
\frac{1}{2 \sqrt{z}}\left[f_X(\sqrt{z}) + f_X(-\sqrt{z})\right],  & z>0 \\
0, & z<0 
\end{cases}
$$ 
